WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:03.870
Film Courage: Why is understanding genre so
important?

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:09.830
John Truby: Genre really is the key to understanding
screenwriting today and it comes from what

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:14.809
I call the First Rule of Hollywood, which
is Hollywood doesn’t buy and sell movie

00:00:14.809 --> 00:00:19.019
stars, directors or writers, it buys and sells
genres.

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:23.359
Now genres are story forms.

00:00:23.359 --> 00:00:27.460
And more importantly they are the all-stars
of the story world.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:34.410
They are story forms that have been told over
decades, centuries and in some cases like

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.120
with myth, thousands of years.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.469
So they have already been worked out.

00:00:39.469 --> 00:00:44.149
They’ve been worked out in very great detail.

00:00:44.149 --> 00:00:49.320
And they know exactly how their characters
work, what their themes are, they know how

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.030
the plot is going to work and so on, so it’s
very, very tough.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:01:03.890
Now in order to sell to a worldwide audience
you have to use these different genres because

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:07.250
this is what the audience is really buying.

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:13.710
When an audience goes to see one of the big
summer blockbuster films, they may not know

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:16.460
in their head “Oh, that’s a myth-based
film.”

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.710
or “That’s an action film.”

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:23.280
or a combination of the two but they know
what they like.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.660
And that’s what is underneath each one of
those stories (AVENGERS, DARK KNIGHT, so on).

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:36.910
Those are all just basic story forms, basic
genres, with a unique skin placed on top.

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:43.980
So it’s very important for the writer to
understand what genres they are working in.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:51.850
It’s also very important for them to understand
minor genres because almost every film that

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:56.090
gets made is not a single genre, that was
decades ago.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:01.830
Now almost every film that gets made (not
just in Hollywood) but in worldwide cinema,

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:07.280
is a combination of two, three and sometimes
four genres.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.860
Now a lot of writers know this.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:16.920
What they don’t know (they usually don’t
know) is that combining genres is very difficult

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:23.560
because each genre has its own hero, it’s
own opponent, it’s own desire line, them

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:24.560
and so on.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.620
So when you start combining these, typically
you end up with chaos.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:35.730
You end up with so many story elements that
are connected that the script doesn’t work.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:40.670
So it’s very important to understand how
to put these genres together.

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:47.590
One final point about this, when a writer
is developing a story idea, right at the beginning

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:53.960
of the writing process they come up against
the biggest decisions that they will have

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:58.070
to make which is “What genre is this story?”

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:05.270
The biggest reason that writers fail at the
premise, and by the way, 99% of scripts fail

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:09.930
at the premise line, right at the biggest
decision in the writing process.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:14.120
The biggest reason is not that it wasn’t
a good idea.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:18.260
It’s that they used the wrong genre to develop
the idea.

